title The publisher a stranger inherits
prompt Keaton: kg on laps C through H.
think Laps C and D land together because they answer one question from two sides -- what does a newcomer inherit, and what do they fill in. C gives them a publisher; D gives both fill-in templates the notation's living name.
think Lap C's shape came from reading publish-seed.sh rather than from the ask. Five literals differ between one publisher and the next: two remote URLs, an identity name and email, and the root-commit subject. Each becomes a stub, each gets a GLOW_PROFILE field of the same name lowercased, so an agent asked to write a publisher from a profile reads one and writes the other.
think The identity stub carries its own reasoning rather than a value: keyless and anonymous ON PURPOSE, because signing a depersonalized projection with your own key links it back to you. A template that only supplied a blank would have taught nothing.
think Lap D is a molt rather than a break. The two tracked templates take .kyri; every reader takes .kyri first and the elder .bron after; .gitignore un-ignores both spellings so a clone carrying an elder file is never silently untracked. That is how the session-log tools already read both, and this pier proves it matters -- GLOW_PROFILE.bron stands on it right now.
obs THE TEMPLATE'S FIRST RUN CAUGHT ITS OWN FAILURE MODE. An unfilled copy refuses before it projects, because a half-filled publisher reaching the push is the one thing this file exists to prevent. Proven by running it: it stops at the first FILL_ME.
obs THE ROOT WILDCARD ATE THE NEW FILE AND THE SEED WITNESS FOUND IT. .gitignore blanket-ignores /* and un-ignores by name, so publish-seed.template.sh sat untracked while the manifest classified it -- sow_witness answered `shipping rows naming a path the tree does not have`. This is the same blanket deny that made publish-seed.sh incidentally untracked (%444), and the manifest coverage check is what turned an invisible omission into a named one.
obs TWO PAIRS I TOUCHED READ AS DRIFTED AND NEITHER DRIFT IS MINE. rule_twin reads 39 against a ceiling of 36 -- and a throwaway worktree at HEAD reads the same 39, so the rise predates this lap. Of the pairs I edited, the-baton and session-log-provenance differ by a single BLANK LINE the normalizer does not eat after stripping frontmatter. Measured rather than assumed, which is why I am not repairing a ceiling I did not move; rule_twin sits at gate %7.
obs MY EDITS WERE SYMMETRIC AND I CHECKED THAT BY COUNTING. Each twin pair took the same insertions and deletions on both sides -- 2/2 and 1/1 -- which is the cheap check that a sed run over two files did the same thing to each.
obs THE GUARD I WROTE THIS LAP CAUGHT MY OWN SWEEP MISSING ITS OWN FILES. The lap-D census was taken BEFORE lap C created publish-seed.template.sh and its two fixtures, so three new files still named GLOW_PROFILE.template.bron and the control died at cp on the hot pass. A census is a photograph of the tree at the moment it was taken, and a round that creates files after taking one has to look again.
